{"timestamp":"2026-05-08T15:59:02.597Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"Yes, this makes sense. The current UI hides two critical pieces of context:\n\n1. **Repo scope**\n   `1 repo` is technically true but not useful. It should say the repo, e.g.\n\n```text\n4 agents · entireio/cli · 4 active days\n```\n\nIf recap is ever unscoped, then it should say something like:\n\n```text\n4 agents · all repos (12) · 8 active days\n```\n\nListing all repo names would require the API to return them; for the current repo-scoped path, CLI already knows the slug.\n\n2. **Time window**\n   The ranges are based on the user’s local timezone at runtime. For `week`, it is not “last 168 hours”; it is the last 7 local calendar days ending at the next local midnight. So switching timezones can change boundaries and therefore counts.\n\nI’d recommend adding a compact context line inside the summary box:\n\n```text\nrepo   entireio/cli\nrange  May 2, 2026 12:00 AM EDT -> May 9, 2026 12:00 AM EDT\n```\n\nThen the summary still reads cleanly:\n\n```text\nLast 7 days\n\nrepo   entireio/cli\nrange  May 2, 2026 12:00 AM EDT -> May 9, 2026 12:00 AM EDT\n\nyou   ...\nteam  ...\n```\n\nFor `day`, this would make it explicit that “today” means local midnight to next local midnight. For `month`, it would show May 1 to June 1, which explains low month numbers early in May. For `90d`, it would show the exact 90-day calendar window.\n\nMy recommendation: implement this first as a CLI-only change:\n- Show `resp.Repo` or the resolved repo slug instead of generic `1 repo`.\n- Show formatted local `since`/`until` boundaries from the API response.\n- Keep `repoCount` only when no specific repo is scoped.\n\nThat should solve the “different terminal windows” confusion without touching the API yet.","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
